{"w":2400,"lines":[{"c":1,"x":0.085992423085745368,"t":"Test 4","y":0.045962398328811771,"w":0.06210139592488606,"h":0.015633342821689489},{"w":0.1338142649332682,"x":0.43719964361281805,"c":1,"y":0.09088410769150268,"h":0.018813179938842461,"t":"READING"},{"y":0.15398230080150399,"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","x":0.086242296768350102,"w":0.30800821940104167,"h":0.014749262536873253,"c":1},{"h":0.023322716997084769,"w":0.81547988891601553,"c":1,"x":0.086098907056260429,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","y":0.18755668159530192},{"w":0.17043121337890621,"y":0.20930232568704477,"x":0.084188910641453493,"h":0.016066406047449688,"c":1,"t":"Passage 1 below."},{"y":0.24657294022252041,"c":1,"x":0.10465800739349296,"t":"The impact of climate change on butterflies in Britain","w":0.79890747070312496,"h":0.026279839934852611},{"x":0.084188904525542327,"w":0.77823410034179696,"t":"According to conservationists, populations of around two thirds of butterfly species","y":0.28924418606977398,"h":0.017540474838211861,"c":1},{"t":"have declined in Britain over the past 40 years. If this trend continues, it might have","x":0.084188904749037952,"y":0.30668604653303166,"w":0.78644765218098955,"h":0.017502744218944488,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"unpredictable knock-on effects for other species in the ecosystem. Butterfly eggs","x":0.084188910451478499,"y":0.32412790700011829,"w":0.76386037190755207,"h":0.017465013599677115},{"h":0.017448720678818863,"x":0.084188910167678724,"y":0.34302325583716653,"c":1,"w":0.75564682006835937,"t":"develop into caterpillars and these insects, which are the second stage in a new"},{"t":"butterfly's lifecycle, consume vast quantities of plant material, and in turn act as prey","h":0.020823901241156295,"c":1,"x":0.084104598585315674,"y":0.35878247043773837,"w":0.79687520345052076},{"t":"for birds as well as bats and other small mammals. Only by arming themselves with an","w":0.81724848429361985,"x":0.084188899429998687,"y":0.37935103257713132,"h":0.015997804512316471,"c":1},{"w":0.81108830769856766,"t":"understanding of why butterfly numbers are down can conservationists hope to halt or","x":0.084188905596022323,"c":1,"h":0.016035535131583845,"y":0.39675516242290054},{"h":0.017271814937085184,"t":"reverse the decline.","c":1,"y":0.41167423683156956,"w":0.18965030670166017,"x":0.08595922374628813},{"t":"Butterflies prefer outdoor conditions to be 'just right', which means neither too hot nor","c":1,"w":0.80307001749674478,"x":0.086134808023941981,"y":0.4475963591763723,"h":0.020192302152470587},{"t":"too cold. Under the conditions of climate change, the temperature at any given time in","h":0.019769019292870782,"c":1,"x":0.082080720832140255,"w":0.81119562784830734,"y":0.46641244663013981},{"y":0.48546511630274747,"t":"summer is generally getting warmer, leaving butterflies with the challenge of how to deal","c":1,"w":0.83162216186523452,"h":0.017484736372235909,"x":0.084188918972046833},{"w":0.79876795450846361,"y":0.50290697685534114,"x":0.084188917742119618,"t":"with this. One of the main ways in which species are ensuring conditions suit them is","c":1,"h":0.015988371857499684},{"w":0.79671457926432276,"h":0.017441860412777732,"x":0.084188911134028127,"t":"by changing the time of year at which they are active and reproduce. Scientists refer","y":0.52034883722245528,"c":1},{"t":"to the timing of such lifecycle events as 'phenology', so when an animal or plant starts","c":1,"y":0.53775811184329858,"x":0.082135517551786844,"w":0.81108830769856777,"h":0.017474446254493903},{"h":0.020280564569793991,"x":0.082090614790017558,"w":0.76396072387695313,"c":1,"y":0.55500096719755942,"t":"to do something earlier in the year than it usually does, it is said to be 'advancing"},{"x":0.082135521896837912,"t":"its phenology'.","w":0.14373716990152996,"h":0.016074981239341368,"c":1,"y":0.57404129767790701},{"c":1,"t":"These advances have been observed already in a wide range of butterflies - indeed,","y":0.61032448394364636,"x":0.084188924342610397,"w":0.80082132975260423,"h":0.017699115044247815},{"c":1,"y":0.62556377102503413,"h":0.020341157491228223,"t":"most species are advancing their phenology to some extent. In Britain, as the average","x":0.084111732164923764,"w":0.81126602172851559},{"h":0.02014368895584151,"c":1,"y":0.64284288841494297,"w":0.79066675821940113,"t":"spring temperature has increased by roughly 0.5 °C over the past 20 years, species","x":0.084137829228954603},{"t":"have advanced by between three days and a week on average, to keep in line with","w":0.7823408508300782,"y":0.66279069769781929,"x":0.084188917398102581,"h":0.017445290264478497,"c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.82751541137695306,"t":"cooler temperatures. Is this a sign that butterflies are well equipped to cope with climate","y":0.68023255813563199,"h":0.017441860412777732,"x":0.084188905622859619},{"h":0.019385141468329481,"c":1,"x":0.084128884636201259,"t":"change, and readily adjust to new temperatures? Or are these populations under","y":0.69739140545760947,"w":0.76398768107096349},{"x":0.084188912288228904,"w":0.82135523478190109,"t":"stress, being dragged along unwillingly by unnaturally fast changes? The answer is still","y":0.71651917377717589,"h":0.01749245410120237,"c":1},{"y":0.73539823025529261,"x":0.084188911912242564,"h":0.017508746459420799,"c":1,"t":"unknown, but a new study is seeking to answer these questions.","w":0.60985626220703126},{"w":0.76180699666341156,"c":1,"t":"First, the researchers pulled together data from millions of records that had been","y":0.77020648947696901,"x":0.084188904024425526,"h":0.016224188790560423},{"t":"submitted by butterfly enthusiasts - people who spend their free time observing the","x":0.084188923825471038,"w":0.78439422607421871,"y":0.78779069778962629,"h":0.016044110323475524,"c":1},{"t":"activities of different species. This provided information on 130 species of butterflies in","x":0.08418890560192703,"y":0.80523255825510665,"h":0.016006379704208151,"w":0.81108830769856766,"c":1},{"y":0.82412790709247186,"w":0.80903493245442704,"h":0.01599008678335001,"c":1,"t":"Great Britain every year for a 20-year period. They then estimated the abundance and","x":0.086242287263284922},{"y":0.84156976746531298,"h":0.017441860412777621,"c":1,"t":"distribution of each species across this time, along with how far north in the country they","x":0.08418891893848629,"w":0.83162216186523419},{"x":0.084188905819521639,"w":0.81930185953776036,"y":0.85899704993513093,"t":"had moved. The data also, crucially, allowed researchers to estimate subtle changes in","c":1,"h":0.017456438407785435},{"t":"what time of the year each species was changing into an adult butterfly.","c":1,"w":0.67556467692057298,"x":0.084188913156756751,"y":0.87640118012860369,"h":0.017494168464413096},{"t":"84","y":0.92005813977636952,"c":1,"x":0.084188912450223558,"h":0.013081394746943587,"w":0.026694043477376306},{"w":0.45882354736328124,"h":0.04166666666666663,"c":1,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","y":0.94166666659226195,"x":0.51176469619230891}],"h":3390}